WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.486
Memory leak prevention depends on clear
ownership and predictable cleanup.

2
00:00:04.566 --> 00:00:08.000
Every allocation should have an intended
owner and a release path.

3
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.208
The owner may be a caller, a structure, a
container, a module,

4
00:00:11.288 --> 00:00:12.575
or a lifecycle function,

5
00:00:12.655 --> 00:00:16.028
but it should be identifiable when the
allocation is introduced.

6
00:00:16.108 --> 00:00:17.825
Error paths, replacement paths,

7
00:00:17.905 --> 00:00:22.443
and partial initialization paths deserve
the same attention as successful execution.

8
00:00:22.523 --> 00:00:24.547
Early returns should not skip cleanup.

9
00:00:24.627 --> 00:00:27.693
Replacing a pointer should not abandon the
old allocation.

10
00:00:27.773 --> 00:00:32.066
Cleanup for partially initialized objects
should release what succeeded without

11
00:00:32.146 --> 00:00:33.740
assuming every field is ready.

12
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.702
Containers, nested allocations, duplicated
strings,

13
00:00:36.782 --> 00:00:38.990
and reallocation require explicit rules.

14
00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.768
A container should say whether it owns its
elements.

15
00:00:41.848 --> 00:00:45.098
A structure cleanup routine should release
every owned field.

16
00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:49.594
Resize logic should preserve the original
allocation on failure and update metadata

17
00:00:49.674 --> 00:00:50.716
only after success.

18
00:00:50.796 --> 00:00:52.452
Tools help detect missed cases,

19
00:00:52.532 --> 00:00:55.475
but design and review prevent many leaks
before testing.

20
00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:59.051
Leak checkers, sanitizers, static
analysis, allocation tracing,

21
00:00:59.131 --> 00:01:03.546
and boundary-focused tests are strongest
when APIs use simple ownership contracts.

22
00:01:03.626 --> 00:01:07.919
Prefer maintainable cleanup rules over
clever assumptions that only the original

23
00:01:07.999 --> 00:01:08.980
author remembers.
